WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.030
Your service has 5,000 tiny tasks.

2
00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:09.348
Run each one on a brand-new thread, and look at what one costs.

3
00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:16.677
Starting a platform thread means a system call to the OS, a new stack, and an entry in the OS scheduler.

4
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.624
Then the task runs, for a few microseconds.

5
00:00:19.624 --> 00:00:21.777
Then all of it is torn down again.

6
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.484
Most of that time goes to the thread, not the work.

7
00:00:25.484 --> 00:00:30.471
Now do it 5,000 times, and compare four threads that are kept and reused.

8
00:00:30.471 --> 00:00:31.769
How big is the gap?

9
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:39.984
On a 4-core machine: half a second to a second and a half with a new thread each time.

10
00:00:39.984 --> 00:00:42.587
6 to 25 ms on four reused threads.

11
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.097
A thread pool fixes this.

12
00:00:45.097 --> 00:00:47.972
It starts a few threads once, and keeps them.

13
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.690
Tasks go into a queue.

14
00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:53.888
Each worker runs one loop: take a task, run it, take the next.

15
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.694
Four workers, so at most four tasks run at once.

16
00:00:57.694 --> 00:01:00.829
When the queue is empty, take blocks, and they wait.

17
00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:02.839
Now the service is stopping.

18
00:01:02.839 --> 00:01:05.676
Five tasks are queued, and four are running.

19
00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.290
main calls shutdown.

20
00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.305
Does it wait for those tasks?

21
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:11.377
No.

22
00:01:11.377 --> 00:01:15.027
shutdown closes the door and returns at once.

23
00:01:15.027 --> 00:01:16.892
A new task is rejected.

24
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.699
The queued and running tasks still finish.

25
00:01:19.699 --> 00:01:23.535
To wait for that, main calls awaitTermination, with a timeout.

26
00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:29.471
When the last worker exits, the pool is terminated, and awaitTermination returns true.

27
00:01:29.471 --> 00:01:32.655
If the timeout runs out first, it returns false.

28
00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:35.625
shutdownNow is the hard stop.

29
00:01:35.625 --> 00:01:41.296
It closes the door, empties the queue, and hands back the five tasks that never ran.

30
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.039
Then it interrupts the four running tasks.

31
00:01:44.039 --> 00:01:45.913
An interrupt is only a request.

32
00:01:45.913 --> 00:01:49.057
Three were blocked in calls that check it, and stop.

33
00:01:49.057 --> 00:01:52.321
One is in a loop that never checks, and keeps running.

34
00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:55.547
The usual idiom puts them together.

35
00:01:55.547 --> 00:01:59.341
shutdown, wait a while, then shutdownNow for the stragglers.

36
00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:00.197
The rule.

37
00:02:00.197 --> 00:02:03.840
shutdown stops new work, and the queue still drains.

38
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.273
shutdownNow also drops the queue, and interrupts.

39
00:02:07.273 --> 00:02:08.533
Neither one waits.

40
00:02:08.533 --> 00:02:10.075
awaitTermination does.

41
00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.062
Check yourself.

42
00:02:12.062 --> 00:02:16.480
main returns, and nobody called shutdown on a fixed pool.

43
00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:17.875
Does the JVM exit?

44
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:20.827
No.

45
00:02:20.827 --> 00:02:23.474
Its workers are not daemon threads.

46
00:02:23.474 --> 00:02:27.255
They wait in take forever, and keep the JVM alive.
